## API Reference

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/webhook` | Receives GitHub webhook events |
| `GET` | `/api/reviews` | Paginated list of reviews |
| `GET` | `/api/reviews/stats` | Dashboard statistics + queue status |
| `GET` | `/api/reviews/:id` | Single review with full inline comments |
| `GET` | `/api/reviews/pr/:owner/:repo/:prNumber` | Lookup review by repo + PR number |
| `GET` | `/api/repos` | All repos with aggregated stats |
| `GET` | `/health` | Server health check |

---

## Interview Talking Points

**"How do you prevent duplicate reviews when GitHub retries webhooks?"**
> GitHub includes an `X-GitHub-Delivery` header — a unique UUID per event. Before queuing a job, I check Redis for that key. If it exists, I skip processing. After a successful review, I set the key with a 24-hour TTL. This makes the entire pipeline idempotent.

**"Why return 200 before processing?"**
> GitHub marks a delivery failed if it doesn't receive a 2xx response within 10 seconds. GPT-4o reviews take 15-30 seconds. Without async decoupling via Redis queue, every review would time out, trigger retries, and create duplicates.

**"How do you verify webhooks are really from GitHub?"**
> GitHub signs every payload with HMAC-SHA256 using a shared secret. I recompute the hash and compare with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` — not regular string comparison, which would leak timing information an attacker could use to brute-force the secret.

**"How would you scale this to 10,000 repos?"**
> The webhook server is stateless — put it behind a load balancer and scale horizontally. Redis queue distributes work naturally across multiple workers. Lambda auto-scales to 1000 concurrent executions. The real bottleneck is OpenAI rate limits — I'd add a token-bucket rate limiter and potentially switch to a fine-tuned open-source model for cost efficiency at scale.

**"What happens if OpenAI is down?"**
> Bull queue has exponential backoff configured — it retries 3 times with 3s, 6s, 12s delays. If all retries fail, the job is moved to a failed queue and the review is saved to MongoDB with `status: 'failed'`. The dashboard shows failed reviews and they can be manually retried.